<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Phayao Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1094 CE – 1338 CE (approx.) · capital Phayao</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern Thai principality on Kwan Phayao lake that allied with Lan Na and Sukhothai in the 13th century before being absorbed by Lan Na.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Northern Thai chronicles say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> was founded in the late 11th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khun-chom-tham">Khun Chom Tham</a>, a prince of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ngoenyang">Ngoenyang</a> line, on the shores of the lake now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwan-phayao">Kwan Phayao</a>. Its early history rests on chronicle tradition.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> was a Tai muang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing valleys whose rulers were linked by kinship to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ngoenyang">Ngoenyang</a> and Nan. Its best-known ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngam-mueang">Ngam Mueang</a>, was an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai of Lan Na</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng of Sukhothai</a>; the three are said to have sworn friendship around 1287, an event commemorated by the Three Kings Monument in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-chiang-mai">Chiang Mai</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngam-mueang">Ngam Mueang's</a> death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> weakened, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a> annexed it in 1338 under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham-fu">Kham Fu</a>. It became a provincial town of Lan Na and later of Siam, and is now the centre of Phayao province in northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/phayao-kingdom.jpg" alt="Phayao Kingdom" width="320" height="258"><figcaption>Image: Ubs6u!d-pongsakorn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phayao_kingdom_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1094 CE – 1338 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phayao</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khun Chom Tham, Ngam Mueang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Northern Thai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Phu Kam Yao, Mueang Phayao</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1216&amp;focus=phayao-kingdom" aria-label="Phayao Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7380 1466 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Phayao Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7700 1722L7697 1683"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7697 1683h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7700 1722h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7700 1722h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Phayao. <a href="/atlas?year=1216&amp;focus=phayao-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
